live-draw: host anti-aliasing pass (FXAA, ENHANCEMENT) on a frame-sized A8R8G8B8 colour surface right before an
NV0039 copy of the whole surface writes it back to guest memory.

Why (Killzone 3): the game anti-aliases with MLAA on the SPUs (post job 0x26, Transpose/MLAA/Transpose/MLAA queued by
the PPU function 0x1FF008 in v1.00 / 0x20A958 in 1.14) in place on the NV0039 copy of the 1280x720 scene in main memory
(local 0x1300000 -> main 0x1310400). MLAA bakes a 1280-pixel pitch (blocks a higher internal resolution) and is one of
the two hottest SPU jobs. With the port skipping that call, live-draw anti-aliases the scene on the GPU where the
copy picks it up, so the SPU Upsample and the composite downstream are unchanged.

What: one compute pass (FXAA 3.11 quality-style: 4-neighbour luma early exit at max(0.0833, 0.166 * lumaMax),
edge direction from the 3x3 luma, 8-step end search 1.5/2/2/2/2/4/8, subpixel term 0.75) from the surface's SRV into
a UAV texture, copied back over the surface. Alpha is passed through; pixels under the contrast threshold are written
back bit-exactly. Once per frame per surface; pipeline built lazily (a compile failure only disables the pass).
Setting: KZ3_HOST_AA=fxaa|off (unset = fxaa). The pass runs when the mode is fxaa and either KZ3_HOST_AA is set
explicitly or the port called rsx_live_draw_host_aa_arm(1) after skipping the game's own AA, so FXAA never stacks
on SPU MLAA. Diagnostic: "[host-aa] fxaa on surface ..." (first 4), rsx_live_draw_debug_host_aa_passes().

Verified: tools/smoke/test_host_aa.c under Proton (rotated 2-colour checker, 1280x720, three frames): flat pixels
875,111 unchanged, alpha unchanged, 73.6 % of 46,489 edge pixels changed, PASS with KZ3_HOST_AA=fxaa and with --arm;
KZ3_HOST_AA=off and unset without --arm FAIL (0 edge pixels changed).

